{% block how_it_works %}
## How it works

A screen of many events splits the users over many edges (or cells), and most of
them are too thin to test: rare events break popular transitions into
fragments, and events that always follow each other split one behaviour into
two edges. `get_ab_compression` proposes a smaller vocabulary for the same
users, one operation at a time, and returns the plan: what to do with every
event, and why. [`compress_events(plan)`](/docs/data-processors/compress-events)
applies it.

### The operations

Each step of the greedy path applies one operation, and the numbers of the
screen after it (cells, testable cells, median MDE, transition mass retained,
artificial transitions) are one row of `attrs["path"]`:

- **merge a chain** `A → B` into `A+B` when `P(next = B | A)` and
  `P(prev = A | B)` both reach `chain_threshold` (pooled): the two events are one
  behaviour. Runs of the merged label collapse into one event, so a merge
  creates no self-loop that its members did not have.
- **drop a rare event** (in fewer than `min_path_share` of the paths) when it
  only interrupts transitions that exist anyway: removing `X` turns `A → X → B`
  into `A → B`, and the screen decides whether that is faithful (below).
- **relabel a rare event to `other`** when dropping it would not be faithful: it
  keeps the event's position and invents no `A → B`.
- **merge synonyms**: two events with the same incoming and outgoing transitions
  (Jensen-Shannon distance at most `synonym_threshold`, no self-loops of their
  own), one of them thin, when the merge leaves fewer untestable cells and loses
  no testable one.

`keep` events, `path_start` / `path_end` and the events of a declared `edges=`
family are never touched. With `max_events`, the rarest events left are then
handled the same way until the vocabulary fits. `target` picks the step: by
default the one with the most testable cells (then the fewest untestable ones,
the lowest median MDE, the earliest step), so a screen that is already testable
keeps the stream as it is (step 0). `step=k` returns the plan at another step.

### One plan per screen

The two screens need different vocabularies, and `screen` builds the plan for
one of them:

- `"transitions"` wants **fewer, richer edges with no artificial transition**: a
  rare event is dropped only when the events around it already follow each other
  directly in at least `min_support` paths for `bridge_threshold` of its runs,
  else it is relabelled `other`; chains are merged first. Every compressed edge
  that no original direct transition maps to is counted in
  `artificial_transitions`.
- `"steps"` wants **aligned steps**: a rare event is dropped when it is an
  insertion (the paths without it go from the event before to the event after
  directly at least as often as through one other event), because dropping it
  moves the later events of its paths back to the step of the paths without it;
  otherwise it is relabelled `other`, which keeps every position. Rare events
  come first, chains last.

On `load_ab_test()` with `min_path_share=0.1`, `support_chat` (8.5 % of the
users) is rare for both screens. All of its runs sit between two events that
also follow each other directly, so the transitions plan drops it; only 57 % of
them are insertions (the paths without it more often take another event at that
place), so the steps plan relabels it `other` to keep the later steps in place.

### Chosen without the arms

Every statistic that drives the plan is computed on the pooled paths of both
arms: the arm of a path is never read, only the two arm sizes (for the MDE).
Choosing the vocabulary from the arms' differences would be selection on the
outcome, and the screen on the compressed stream would no longer control its
false discoveries; filtering on a statistic that is independent of the arms
under the null, as the pooled support is, keeps Benjamini-Hochberg valid
("independent filtering", Bourgon, Gentleman and Huber, 2010). Permuting the
arm labels, or swapping the arms in `diff`, leaves the plan identical. Fix the
plan before reading the screen it is for, and declare the events the treatment
introduces or changes in `keep`: the plan cannot see the arms, so it cannot tell
a synonym from the treatment's replacement of an event.

### Homogeneity

With `by=<a segment fixed before exposure>`, every segment gets its own plan (and
the segment column on every row), and `attrs["homogeneity"]` a summary per
segment: the effective vocabulary size (`exp` of the entropy of the event
distribution), the transition entropy in bits, and the share of distinct paths.
A homogeneous segment (a few events carry most of the paths, many users share a
path) reaches enough testable cells with little compression; a heterogeneous
one needs more. The segment must be fixed before exposure, as for the
scorecard's strata: pass `by_pre_exposure=True`, or the stream's recipe must
show it.

### On the bundled datasets

The bundled experiments are dense: their default plans keep the stream as it is,
except `session_assignment`, whose sessions all open with `session_start →
home` (one chain merge: 57 of 59 step cells testable before, 65 of 66 after, as
every later event moves one step earlier into the window). A cap forces a real
compression: on `load_ab_test()`, `max_events=10` with `keep=["quick_add",
"cart"]` merges `checkout_start+payment` and drops four rare events
(`support_chat`, `search_filter`, `collections_view`, `wishlist_add`); all 58
edges of the compressed screen are testable (90 of 92 before), and its 19
discoveries include the planted ones (`cart → cart`, `product_view → quick_add`,
`quick_add → cart`). Compression is not free: the dropped events took 24.5 % of
the transitions with them (`mass_retained` 0.755), and the median MDE of the
testable edges rose from 2.03 to 2.52 pp, as the best-measured rare edges left
the family. With `screen="steps"` the same cap relabels those events `other`
instead (and `order_success`), which keeps every position. No default plan on a
bundled dataset creates an artificial transition.

